diff --git a/docs/RSPEC_PERFORMANCE.md b/docs/RSPEC_PERFORMANCE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..80e0328
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/RSPEC_PERFORMANCE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,197 @@
+# RSpec performance notes
+
+## Measuring a phase
+
+Run one spec directory at a time, serially, against a prepared test database
+and Redis. Keep the same seed, Ruby, service configuration, and CI/coverage
+settings for the before and after runs. For the model phase, from the repo root:
+
+```bash
+mkdir -p tmp/model-perf
+(time bundle exec rspec spec/models --seed 41729 --profile 12) \
+  > tmp/model-perf/models.log 2>&1
+```
+
+Use the RSpec `Finished in` line for example execution time and `time` for
+wall time (including boot). Check the example and failure counts. Leave the
+normal tag exclusions in place. Unset `COVERAGE` for a non-coverage run;
+setting it to `false` still enables it. Use a separate run for instrumentation
+so profiler overhead isn't included in the comparison:
+
+```bash
+TEST_STACK_PROF=1 TEST_STACK_PROF_RAW=0 \
+  EVENT_PROF=sql.active_record FPROF=1 RD_PROF=1 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/models --seed 41729 --require test-prof --profile 12 \
+  > tmp/model-perf/profile.log 2>&1
+bundle exec stackprof tmp/test_prof/stack-prof-report-wall-total.dump --limit 25
+```
+
+## Phase 1: models
+
+Local serial measurements (Ruby 4.0.6, PostgreSQL 17, Redis 8, CI/coverage
+unset, seed 41729). These are not estimates of CI job time.
+
+| Configuration                                 | RSpec execution | Wall time |
+| --------------------------------------------- | --------------: | --------: |
+| Baseline                                      |           170 s |   173.5 s |
+| Remove verbose query logging                  |          94.6 s |    97.8 s |
+| Also switch models to fake Sidekiq by default |          64.3 s |    67.7 s |
+
+The final run executed the same 1,795 examples and took **62% less time** by
+RSpec's timer (**61% less wall time**, including boot).
+
+A second run with `CI=true COVERAGE=true` and seed 19031 also passed all
+1,795 examples. A focused run with `--tag sidekiq:inline` passed all four
+examples that check queued email delivery or non-delivery.
+
+The baseline profile identified setup as the main cost: RSpecDissect attributed
+137 of 168 seconds to setup. EventProf measured 26.1 seconds across 113,961
+`sql.active_record` events; query execution alone didn't explain the runtime.
+In StackProf, 33.6% of wall samples were in SQL caller backtrace cleanup and
+23.0% were in `TriggerWebhookWorker#perform`, mostly serializing events during
+fixture setup. These inclusive counts overlap and aren't additive.
+
+- `Status::FetchRepliesConcern` enabled `ActiveRecord.verbose_query_logs` while
+  its spec file was being loaded. This made Rails find and print the Ruby caller
+  for SQL queries in unrelated examples. The spec doesn't assert on logging, so
+  it now leaves Rails' normal logging alone. If you need query caller logging
+  when debugging, don't enable it at spec-file load time.
+- By default, `spec/models` uses Sidekiq's fake mode: callbacks still enqueue
+  jobs, but setup doesn't run unrelated worker bodies synchronously. For
+  examples that assert a worker's effects (including delivery of mail queued
+  via Active Job), use `sidekiq: :inline` on the example or group. Keep
+  non-delivery assertions inline too when the purpose is to prove that no mail
+  is sent. At this phase, other directories kept the inline default. The existing
+  rspec-sidekiq hook clears fake jobs before each example; database, cache, and
+  Redis cleanup are unchanged.
+
+## Phase 2: requests
+
+Use the same local setup and seed as phase 1. Measure the directory alone:
+
+```bash
+mkdir -p tmp/request-perf
+(time bundle exec rspec spec/requests --seed 41729 --profile 15) \
+  > tmp/request-perf/requests.log 2>&1
+```
+
+For a separate profile, use the TestProf command above with `spec/requests`
+in place of `spec/models`. The request baseline was taken after phase 1 but
+before any request changes. The old verbose-query setting only leaked if the
+model spec that set it was loaded, and phase 1's Sidekiq setting was scoped to
+models. Neither changed this standalone request baseline.
+
+Local serial measurements with Ruby 4.0.6, PostgreSQL 17, and Redis 8,
+seed 41729, and CI/coverage unset:
+
+| Request configuration | RSpec execution | Wall time |
+| --------------------- | --------------: | --------: |
+| Baseline              |         176.9 s |   180.9 s |
+| Optimized             |          94.8 s |    98.0 s |
+
+Both runs passed the same 1,877 examples. Request execution took **46% less
+time**. The two directories' separate execution times add up to **346.9 s →
+159.1 s (2.18× as fast)** using the phase 1 model measurements. This compares
+local serial runs, not whole-suite or CI job times.
+
+The baseline profile pointed to common setup rather than slow HTTP actions:
+
+- RSpecDissect measured 150.5 s of setup across 184.6 s of example time. The
+  cache matrix alone spent 35.8 s in setup across 258 examples; it used to
+  rebuild the same accounts, statuses, poll, invite, and token for each case.
+- EventProf measured 39.0 s across 237,898 SQL events. In StackProf,
+  Sidekiq's test client ran in 28.5% of wall samples, including 21.1% in
+  `TriggerWebhookWorker#perform`. The worker serializes records even when
+  there are no webhooks to deliver. SQL debug logging took 7.0% inclusive.
+  Sample counts overlap; do not add these percentages. Warden's `set_user`
+  took 0.6%, so no changes to sign-in or authentication helpers were needed.
+
+Changes:
+
+- `spec/requests` now uses fake Sidekiq by default, just like `spec/models`.
+  Tag the narrowest group or example that needs worker results with
+  `sidekiq: :inline`. This includes mail delivery (and assertions about
+  non-delivery), feeds and conversations populated by workers in setup,
+  notification setup, and asynchronous unfavourites. Fake-mode jobs are
+  still enqueued and cleared by rspec-sidekiq before the next example.
+- Request examples suppress `DEBUG` Rails logs, including SQL bind formatting,
+  without disabling SQL notifications. The wrapper keeps `INFO` and higher
+  and restores the previous level. Set `RSPEC_DEBUG_LOGS=1` to get debug
+  output when investigating a failure, or use `quiet_rails_logs: false` for
+  a spec that needs the original log level.
+- The cache matrix shares its base database data in a TestProf `before_all`
+  transaction. Examples refetch the records and still run in their own
+  rollback scope with fresh request sessions, cache, and Redis state. Do not
+  move sign-in, mode changes, or Redis-backed setup into `before_all`.
+  Shared setup runs outside the per-example Sidekiq hook, so it explicitly
+  uses a block-scoped fake mode. Across the baseline and optimized runs,
+  reported example times for the cache matrix sum to 35.6 s and 3.7 s,
+  respectively.
+
+A trial of fake jobs with no inline exceptions surfaced 41 failures. Those
+failures identified job dependencies that the final metadata preserves.
+
+The alternate-seed run (`19031`, `CI=true COVERAGE=true`, with CAS, SAML,
+and OIDC enabled) passed all 1,895 examples. A separate run with
+`RSPEC_DEBUG_LOGS=1` and `--tag sidekiq:inline` passed all 52 tagged request
+examples. The verification also checked that logging levels were restored
+and the shared cache fixtures were rolled back.
+
+## Leads from phases 1 and 2
+
+- Factory definitions were left unchanged. Baseline FactoryProf counted 4,721
+  creations, with 1,019 bookmarks but only 17 created at the top level. Inspect
+  nested creation in the factory phase. Use RSpecDissect to distinguish repeated
+  example setup from the code under test. In the baseline,
+  `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy`, `Trends::Statuses`, `TagFeed`, and `PublicFeed`
+  had expensive repeated record graphs. Sharing setup is most straightforward
+  for database-only data; Redis-backed state still needs per-example isolation.
+  `MediaAttachment` was the slowest file after the changes (6.3 s across 27
+  examples); keep real media processing in tests that verify its output.
+- In the requests baseline FactoryProf counted 11,030 creations, including
+  1,459 bookmarks (only 9 top-level) and 2,970 login activities (none
+  top-level). Re-profile before changing factories because skipping inline
+  workers and sharing cache setup have already removed some of this cost.
+- In the optimized request run, the largest files by total example time were
+  ActivityPub Replies (8.7 s), signature verification (7.5 s), and the V2
+  and V1 notifications specs (7.1 s and 5.2 s). Review repeated database
+  setup first. Keep real signing, requests, and needed worker effects in
+  cases that cover them.
+
+## Phase 3: factory graphs
+
+The status fabricator's implicit bookmark also builds a separate local account
+and user. The user fabricator creates two login activities, including for
+those extra bookmark accounts. Most model and request examples need neither
+side effect. Keep the actual status author, user account, and other required
+associations intact.
+
+Model and request examples now run with these optional records disabled by
+default. For a spec that needs them, explicitly fabricate the related records
+with the account/status/user it cares about, or use `with_bookmark: true` on
+`:status` or `with_login_activities: true` on `:user` (and its derivatives) to
+request the old factory behavior. The scoped default is restored even if an
+example raises. Other directories and context-level shared setup outside the
+example wrapper retain the previous defaults.
+
+To measure the removed fan-out, run FactoryProf separately for each directory
+with the same seed, using `FPROF=1` and `--require test-prof` as above. Compare
+total and top-level counts, not just the time charged to the nested factories
+(their inclusive times overlap).
+For a legacy-default comparison on the same checkout, temporarily disable the
+`:lean_fabricators` around hook while leaving the Sidekiq and logging settings
+unchanged. The phase 3 model baseline had 4,721 factory creations; the lean
+run had 3,449 (27% fewer), with the same 3,243 top-level calls. Implicit
+bookmarks fell from 1,002 to zero, and login activities from 270 to zero.
+All 1,795 model examples and all 1,877 request examples passed with lean
+factories at seed 41729. The shared request cache setup accounts for the two
+remaining implicit bookmarks and two login activities in the lean run.
+
+In the request comparison, factory creations fell from 8,203 to 4,815
+(41% fewer), with 4,762 top-level calls in both runs. Bookmarks fell from
+945 to 11 and login activities from 2,456 to 2. RSpec execution time with
+FactoryProf enabled fell from **93.88 s to 84.48 s (10% less time)**.
+The serial model rerun with FactoryProf took **47.40 s**, down from
+**60.70 s (22% less time)**. Across the separately executed directories,
+the profiled total fell from **154.58 s to 131.88 s (15% less time)**.
+These are local RSpec execution times excluding boot, not CI job timings.
